# Copy this file to .env and adjust if you need to. Every value has a working
# default, so an empty .env (or no .env at all) runs correctly on this Mac.
# .env is gitignored and must never be committed.

# All of these are read by docker compose, not by the app. cmd-center itself
# takes no secrets and no API keys.

# Host port the container is published on. The default is 3201, NOT 3200.
#
# 3200 belongs to the host instance: the Makefile, cmdc.sh, the LaunchAgent and
# ~/repo/appmenu all assume it, and the host instance is the only one that can
# build the macOS projects. So out of the box the two run side by side and the
# container never takes the port you actually depend on.
#
# Changing this is safe: compose feeds it into ALLOWED_ORIGINS so the origin
# check follows the port. Setting it anywhere else without that would make every
# terminal fail with a 403 on the WebSocket while the page still loaded.
#
# Set HOST_PORT=3200 only if you want the container INSTEAD of the host
# instance, and run "make stop" first.
# HOST_PORT=3201

# Interface the published port binds to on the Mac. Loopback only. Do not point
# this at a LAN address: the app hands out an interactive shell, and the
# container has no authentication in front of it.
# BIND_ADDR=127.0.0.1

# The host directory mounted into the container, at that same path inside it.
# Everything cmd-center is allowed to open a terminal in must live under here.
# REPO_MOUNT=/Users/michael/repo

# Roots scanned for projects with a Makefile, colon-separated, in precedence
# order. An earlier root wins a name collision. These are container paths, which
# are the same as the host paths because of how REPO_MOUNT is mounted.
# REPO_ROOTS=/Users/michael/repo/claude:/Users/michael/repo

# Account the container runs as. 1000:1000 is the node user built into the
# image. Leave it alone unless you have a reason: an arbitrary uid has no passwd
# entry inside the image, which leaves the browser shell with no username and no
# home directory. OrbStack remaps bind-mount ownership to whatever uid runs, so
# 1000 does not limit file access.
# APP_UID=1000
# APP_GID=1000

# TZ=America/New_York
